diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase sweep of the lab found no fault at any layer. Phase 1 confirmed ICMP reachability is 100% (0% loss) between all tested host pairs, including `host_1_1_1_1 → web_server_0` (10.200.0.3). Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found no ACL/firewall rules, no addressing anomaly, no duplicate MACs, and confirmed both hosts hold valid IPs and default routes. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) found all six FRR routers alive with vtysh reachable, all OSPF adjacencies at Full state, 10 routes propagated on every router, and no traffic-shaping qdiscs anywhere in the fabric. Phase D (service_snapshot + pressure_sweep) confirmed that the DNS server (10.200.0.2) correctly resolves `web0.local → 10.200.0.3` from both clients, that `http://web0.local/` returns HTTP 200 to `host_1_1_1_1` in 5 ms, that `web_server_0` answers its own localhost probe in under 1 ms, and that no stress tool, CPU spike, or socket saturation is present on any device. The user's assertion that web_server_0 has "crashed" or that the DNS server has "stopped handing out addresses" is contradicted by all direct evidence. The web page and name resolution are fully operational. The reported outage was either a brief past transient that has since self-resolved, or a mistaken client-side perception (browser cache issue, stale tab, local machine problem). No corrective action is needed on the lab infrastructure.
